@meta
id: 03-03
title: Drill — both ends of the line
chapter: 03
type: drill
skills: insert-I, insert-A
reps: 8
par: 24
minutes: 5
vscode: two clicks and two bursts of typing
open: order.entity.ts

@brief
Eight times: make the field readonly and terminate it. `readonly ` goes in
front of the code, the semicolon goes at the end.

Two trips into insert mode, and the cursor never moves along the line
under its own steam. Nothing to work out. The point is that your fingers
stop choosing between `I` and `A` and just know.

@keys
| `I` | insert at the first non-blank character |
| `A` | append at the end of the line |
| `Esc` | out |
| `:wq` | save and quit |

@hints
`I` first, then `Esc`, then `A` — you do not need to move between them,
because `A` finds the end of the line on its own.
--
By rep four, stop looking at the screen between the two edits. `I` … `Esc`
`A` … `Esc` is one shape, not two decisions.

@solution
    j I readonly<Space> <Esc> A ; <Esc> :wq<Enter>

Nineteen keystrokes, ten of them the word `readonly` and the semicolon.

What you are drilling is the absence of a step. There is no "get to the
start of the line" and no "get to the end of the line" in that sequence,
because both are baked into the capitals. A VS Code habit puts a mouse
click or a Home/End press between every edit and the next; this does not.

When you meet `C` and `S` in later chapters they follow the same rule:
the capital is the whole-line-flavoured version of the lowercase key.

@start order.entity.ts
export type Order = {
  id: string
};

@expected order.entity.ts
export type Order = {
  readonly id: string;
};
